window.__saqPaperQuestions("2019-2-pah",[{"number":1,"title":"","stemText":"A 65 year old man has been referred by his GP with a two week history of increasing confusion. Bloods done by the GP reveal a serum sodium of 110 mmol/L (reference range 135 - 145 mmol/L).","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List 4 historical features you would seek in determining the UNDERLYING CAUSE of this\npatient’s hyponatremia. Include the clinical relevance. (4 marks) Historical feature Clinical relevance"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List 4 examination findings you would seek in determining the UNDERLYING CAUSE of this\npatient’s hyponatremia. Include the clinical relevance. (4 marks) Examination finding Clinical relevance"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List 4 investigations you would perform to help determine the UNDERLYING CAUSE of this\npatient’s hyponatremia. Justify your choices. (4 marks) Investigation Justification While waiting for admission to an inpatient medical ward, the patient has been found lying next to his bed. He is complaining of severe right hip pain. An x-ray of his pelvis is provided in the PROPS BOOKLET."},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"Describe the abnormalities on the x-ray. (3 marks) You have decided to perform a femoral nerve block."},{"id":"e","label":"Part E","prompt":"Complete the table by listing the maximum safe doses for the local anaesthetic agents given. (2 marks) Agent Maximum Dose Lignocaine Bupivicaine The patient’s daughter has arrived at the ED. She is very upset with her father’s condition and treatment."},{"id":"f","label":"Part F","prompt":"State 6 important features of your discussion with the patient’s daughter. (6 marks)\n1)________________________________________________________________________________ 2)________________________________________________________________________________ 3)________________________________________________________________________________ 4)________________________________________________________________________________ 5)________________________________________________________________________________ 6)________________________________________________________________________________"}],"propPages":["asset:img70f1390ff6bd075b5b"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Medications – new meds or changes in dose of drugs that are associated with hyponatraemia (ie</div><div class=\"answer-line\">thiazides, SSRIs, anticonvulsants)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">GIT illness with volume loss – could produce hypovolaemic hyponatraemia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Hx of liver disease (or surrogate ie increasing abdominal distention – hypervolaemic hyponatraemia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Hx of CCF – hypervolaemic hyponatraemia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Hx excessive water intake – ie psychogenic polydipsia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Hx malignancy (lung or brain) – risk for development of SIADH</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Hx of weight loss, nightsweats etc – possible undiagnosed malignancy</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Hx of previous hyponatraemia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Really anything here could be ok ie Hx of thyroid dysfunction</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Alcohol intake – seeking potomania</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Volume status (ie accept one of – peripheral oedema, ascites, pulmonary congestion) as evidence of</div><div class=\"answer-line\">hypervolaemic hypontraemia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">ALSO accept one of significant dehydration ie tachycardia, tachypnoea, dry mucous membranes etc</div><div class=\"answer-line\">as evidence of possible hypovolaemic hyponatraemia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Pulmonary findings of malignancy ie focal consolidative signs/crackles, unilateral effusion or better</div><div class=\"answer-line\">answers to be more specific to pulmonary malignancy ie unilateral hand wasting, HPOA, brachial</div><div class=\"answer-line\">plexopathy or Horner’s syndrome to suggest apical lung malignancy</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Goitre or other signs of thyroid disease (expothalmus, tremor)</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">TFTs – hypernatraemia causally linked to SIADH</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Other electrolytes ie if K, Cl also low suggestive of potomania</div><div class=\"answer-line\">(note not accepting osmolarity or BSL as not useful in determining cause)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">BNP would be weak here</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Renal function and/or LFTs likely acceptable if justified through cause of hypervolaemic</div><div class=\"answer-line\">hyponatraemia (not just Chem20)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Urine osmol/Na – useful in identifying sodium wasting of SIADH</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Cortisol – if suspected hypoadrenalism</div><div class=\"answer-line\">CXR – seek malignancy/ other pulmonary process (abscess/empyema) – must state one path process</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Other – CT Head – malignancy/abscess/ infarct</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Sputum cytology – must state seek small cell lung cancer</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Comminuted fracture Rt femur</div><div class=\"answer-line\">-</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Intertrochanteric fracture</div><div class=\"answer-line\">-</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Spiral fracture of proximal femoral shaft with shortening and displacement</div><div class=\"answer-line\">(one mark for description – displacement/angulation )</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part E</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Lignocaine - accept 3-5mg/kg</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Bupivicane – accept 2-2.5mg/kg</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part F</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Open disclosure of events around fall and femoral fracture</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Apologise for events and injuries sustained in hospital</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Explain that incidents will be investigated through hospital processes</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Ensure understanding of severity of father’s presentation – severe hyponatraemia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Explanation of need for fixation of femoral fracture</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Seek clarification around father’s history and premorbid level of function</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Seek clarification around pre-existing documentation around advanced health directives etc</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":2,"title":"","stemText":"A 12 month old boy has been brought to the ED with abdominal pain and vomiting.\nYour junior doctor has ordered an abdominal x-ray which is shown in the PROPS BOOKLET.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"State 3 abnormalities on the x-ray. (3 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"State the likely diagnosis. (1 mark)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List 2 options for analgesia in this patient, including doses. The child weighs 10kg.\n(2 marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"List 2 options for definitive management of this condition. (2 marks)"},{"id":"e","label":"Part E","prompt":"List 3 possible complications of this condition. (3 marks)"}],"propPages":["asset:imgf0641b0ca7fdad6357"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Two large, focally dilated loops of small bowel</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Paucity of bowel gas</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Crescent sign with soft tissue mass projecting into dilated loop of bowel</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Intussusception</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">I/N fentanyl 1.5mcg/kg ie 15mcg</div><div class=\"answer-line\">IV Morphine 0.1mg/kg</div><div class=\"answer-line\">IV Fentanyl 1mcg/kg</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Air enema (radiologic)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Surgical</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part E</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Bowel Ischaemia/necrosis</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Bowel Perforation with peritonitis</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Vomiting with aspiration</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Shock with cardiovascular collapse</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":3,"title":"","stemText":"You are in charge of a shift in your tertiary ED. There are 16 patients ramped in the triage corridor.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List 6 potential causes for this problem. (6 marks)\n1)________________________________________________________________________________ 2)________________________________________________________________________________ 3)________________________________________________________________________________ 4)________________________________________________________________________________ 5)________________________________________________________________________________ 6)________________________________________________________________________________"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"State 5 steps you will take to address the situation. (5 marks)\n1)________________________________________________________________________________ 2)________________________________________________________________________________ 3)________________________________________________________________________________ 4)________________________________________________________________________________ 5)________________________________________________________________________________"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Demand ie Surge (ie major event)/ Pandemic</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Lack of primary health care availability (ie public holidays/ long weekend)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Other health services on ‘bypass’ or redirect (due to internal disaster)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Lack of senior staff</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Delayed decision-making</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Delay to inpatient review</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Absolute staff deficiency/sick leave/ rostering etc</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Delayed turn around of investigations/reporting (pathology/ radiology)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Lack of alternate treatment spaces (ie SSW at capacity, lack of ambulatory care space)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">ED treatment areas full of admitted patients</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Hospital occupancy over census</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Delays to discharge of inpatients</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Poor discharge planning</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Lack of alternate services ie Hospital In The Home, early outpatient clinics</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Notify ambulance services to consider load-sharing</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Notify ED/hospital executive to activate hospital wide processes such as Code Yellow/ Disaster Plan</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Assign staff to review ramped patients and ensure deterioriating/critically ill are identified</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Clear ED treatment space by admitting suitable patients directly to ward, moving suitable patients to</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Short Stay or alternate space ie waiting room for ambulant patients awaiting test</div><div class=\"answer-line\">results/prescriptions</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Ensure adequate staffing – call in on-call staff if staffing deficiency</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Early rounding in ED to ensure early decision making</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":4,"title":"","stemText":"A 45 year old woman has been brought to the ED after an overdose of multiple drugs. Your registrar asks for your opinion on whether the patient has serotonin toxicity.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List 3 groups of drugs that can cause serotonin toxicity. Include one drug from each group. (3 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List 6 examination features that would be consistent with serotonin toxicity.\n(6 marks) Your assessment is consistent with significant serotonin toxicity."},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List 3 drugs that may be used to manage serotonin toxicity. (3 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Antidepressants:</div><div class=\"answer-line\">MAOIs - any</div><div class=\"answer-line\">SSRIs - any</div><div class=\"answer-line\">SNRIs - any</div><div class=\"answer-line\">TCAs - any</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Opioids – tramadol, buprenorphine</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Anticonvulsants (Valproate, Carbamazepine)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">5-HT3 antagonists - ondansetron</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Metoclopramide</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Lithium</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Amphetamines</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Antibiotics – ciprofloxacin, erythromycin</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Exam features:</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Cognitive – agitation, confusion, convulsions, coma</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Autonomic – shivering, diaphoresis, fever, tachycardia, nausea, diarrhoea</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Somatic – myoclonus (LL &gt;UL), ocular clonus, hyperrflexia, tremor</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Benzodiazepines ie diazepam</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Serotonin Antagonists – Cyproheptadine, Chlorpromazine, olanzapine</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":5,"title":"","stemText":"You are working in the short stay unit of your ED. You are about to see a 25 year old man who was brought to the ED the previous evening with abnormal behaviour and suicidal ideation. He was diagnosed with amphetamine related agitation and was managed with parenteral sedation. He is currently expressing a desire to leave.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"State 5 features of your examination that would suggest ongoing amphetamine\nintoxication. (5 marks) You have decided that he is no longer intoxicated with amphetamines."},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"State 5 features of your mental status examination that would indicate a high risk of\nsuicide. (5 marks) You have assessed the patient as requiring involuntary treatment due to high suicide risk. He is now agitated and about to leave."},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List 2 drugs, with doses, that could be used as chemical restraint. (2 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Agitation/Anxiety/ Hypervigilence</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Paranoia/Hallucinations</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Psychomotor agitation – restlessness, tremor</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Pressured speech</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Sweating, flushed skin</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Teeth Grinding, Jaw clenching</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Mydriasis</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Hypertension</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Tachycardia</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Anything from SADPERSONS really:</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Examples:</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Hopelessness</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Despair</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Anger</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Psychosis</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Lack of engagement</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Poor support network</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Financial stress, unemployment</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Major stress – legal prosecution or child custody issues</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Recent major loss/trauma or anniversary</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Stated plan</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Access to firearms</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Drug/Alcohol dependence or withdrawal</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Droperidol 10mg IM/IV</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Lorazepam IM/IV 2mg</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Ketamine 4mg/kg IM</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Also accept:</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Midazolam 5mg IM</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Olanzapine 10mg IM</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Halperidol 5-10mg IM/IV</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":6,"title":"","stemText":"A 5 year old boy has been brought to the ED with a two day history of fever, sore throat and rash. A clinical photograph has been provided in the PROPS BOOKLET.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List 3 features of the rash. (3 marks)\n1)________________________________________________________________________________ 2)________________________________________________________________________________ 3)________________________________________________________________________________ Examination of his oropharynx reveals erythema and oedema of the soft palate with exudate on both tonsils."},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"What is the most likely diagnosis? (1 mark)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List 2 differential diagnoses. (2 marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"State your treatment of this condition. (2 marks)"},{"id":"e","label":"Part E","prompt":"List 4 potential complications of this condition. (4 marks)"}],"propPages":["asset:img57ecf96cf1dea06c3d"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Erythematous rash</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Macular (accept morbiliform)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Widespread with areas of confluence on trunk and limbs</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Scarlet Fever</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Measles (Rubeola)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Rubella</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Enterovirus</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Drug Rash (ie EBV with amoxicillin)</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Analgesia, antipyretics, hydration doses needed</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part E</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Renal failure</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Rheumatic heart disease</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Abscess – peritonsillar or retropharyngeal</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Bacteraemia/ Sepsis</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Pneumonia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Hepatitis</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":7,"title":"","stemText":"A 30 year old woman has been brought to the ED with severe shortness of breath. She has been progressively getting worse over the last 2 days. She has a history of Goodpasture’s syndrome and her medications include prednisone and cyclophosphamide. A chest x-ray is provided in the PROPS BOOKLET.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List 3 descriptive features of the pathology demonstrated on the x-ray. (3 marks)\n1)________________________________________________________________________________ 2)________________________________________________________________________________ 3)________________________________________________________________________________"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List 3 differential diagnoses. (3 marks)\nThe patient has the following vital signs:\n\nGCS\nE4 V4 M6 Pulse 135 /min\n\nBP\n90/40 mmHg O2 sats 85% 15L O2 via non-rebreather mask You have decided that the patient needs to be intubated as part of your further management."},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"State 6 key aspects of your strategy in the intubation of this patient. (6 marks)\n1)________________________________________________________________________________ 2)________________________________________________________________________________ 3)________________________________________________________________________________ 4)________________________________________________________________________________ 5)________________________________________________________________________________ 6)________________________________________________________________________________"}],"propPages":["asset:imgd559a62f37b5080656"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Bilateral</div><div class=\"answer-line\">patchy alveolar opacities</div><div class=\"answer-line\">both lung fields – widespread distribution</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Pulmonary Haemorrhage (goodpastures)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">LRTI ie staph pneumonia, influenza</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Opportunistic infection (immunosuppressed) – ie Pneumocystis</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Cryptogenic Organising Pneumonia/ Bronchiolitis Obliterans Organising Pneumonia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Congestive Cardiac Failure</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Malignancy with lymphatic dissemination ie Lymphangitis Carcinomatosis</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Steps in Intubation:</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Fluid load – 0.9% saline 10ml/kg bolus, repeat to SBP &gt;100mmHg </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Augmented induction agent – ie ketamine IV at reduced dose ie 0.5-1mg/kg, rocuronium 1.2mg/kg</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Co-administration of inotrope at induction – 1mcg/kg adrenaline with induction</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Optimised pre-oxygenation</strong> with ongoing NRBM at 15lpm PLUS NP O2 at 15lpm</div><div class=\"answer-line\">NP O2 at 15lpm throughout induction stage</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Mitigate hypoxia/acidosis by bagging through induction with BVM O2 at 15lpm</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Intubate at 30 degrees to minimise risk of hypoxia</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":8,"title":"","stemText":"A 12 year old girl has been taken to the ED after a syncopal episode at school.\nOn arrival, she has no ongoing symptoms and normal vital signs.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List 4 findings in your history that would be concerning for a cardiac cause of syncope.\n(4 marks) 1)________________________________________________________________________________ 2)________________________________________________________________________________ 3)________________________________________________________________________________ 4)________________________________________________________________________________"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List 4 findings in your history that would be consistent with vasovagal syncope. (4 marks) 1)________________________________________________________________________________ 2)________________________________________________________________________________ 3)________________________________________________________________________________ 4)________________________________________________________________________________"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List 4 investigations you might perform in the ED. Provide a clinical indication for each\ntest. (4 marks) Investigation Clinical indication"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Unheralded</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Seated or supine</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Exertional</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Fam Hx of sudden cardiac death</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Personal History of prior unexplained syncope</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Congenital cardiac disease</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Heralded Syncope (lightheadedness, diaphoresis)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Inciting event (ie visual, pain, fear)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Post syncopal vagal symptoms – diaphoresis, nausea/vomiting</div><div class=\"answer-line\">History of similar events</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Response / justification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Chem20/ (or potassium) – suspected eating disorder</td><td>CXR – murmur or signs of cardiac failure</td></tr><tr><td>Hb/ FBE – History of menorrhagia/ signs of anaemia</td><td>Urine MCS – history of fever or dysuria or equivalent</td></tr><tr><td>CT Head – only if signs of trauma indicating possible TBI ie signs of BOS fracture complicating fall OR</td><td>lateralising neurology/signs of raised ICP</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":9,"title":"","stemText":"A 25 year old man has presented with a red eye. You have diagnosed him with conjunctivitis.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Complete the table regarding the causes of conjunctivitis. (12 marks) Cause of conjunctivitis Discriminatory features on history / Treatment examination"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><thead><tr><th>Cause</th><th>Distinguishing feature</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Bacterial</td><td>Unilateral</td></tr><tr><td>Dryness of the eye and surrounding skin</td><td>Purulent discharge</td></tr><tr><td>TREATMENT: Antibiotic drops/ointment</td><td>Viral</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":10,"title":"","stemText":"A 3 year old boy has been brought to the ED with anaphylaxis related to an inadvertent ingestion of nuts at a day-care facility. The child has an urticarial rash and wheeze with normal vital signs.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"State your most important initial management step. (1 mark) Despite your initial management, the child has become more unwell.\nHis vital signs are:\nAVPU alert Pulse 180 /min\n\nBP\n65/40 mmHg\n\nRR\n/min O2 sats 90% room air He has worsening wheeze and looks pale."},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"State 4 components of your further management of this child. (4 marks)\n1) ________________________________________________________________________________ 2) ________________________________________________________________________________ 3) ________________________________________________________________________________ 4) ________________________________________________________________________________"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List 4 indications for intubation that might arise in this patient. (4 marks)\nYour management was successful and there was no requirement for airway intervention. He was admitted for overnight observation."},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"List 3 components of your discharge plan with respect to Epipen prescription. (3 marks)\n1) ________________________________________________________________________________ 2) ________________________________________________________________________________ 3) ________________________________________________________________________________"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">IM Adrenaline (Epipen Jr) or 0.15mg</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">O2 ie via NRB mask to target Sats &gt;94, decreased resp distress</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Steroid – ie HCT 4mg/kg IV</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Refractory hypoxia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Cardiac arrest</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Upper airway obstruction</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Apnoea/ loss of respiratory drive</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Coma or obtundation</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Refractory shock</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Ensure adequate education re: technique (parental education)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Script must include at least two pens for different locations – ie home/school/car etc</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Letter for school to ensure they are aware and can store +/- administer Epipen</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":11,"title":"","stemText":"A 58 year old woman has presented to the ED with confusion. She has a background of chronic liver disease due to alcoholism.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List the components of the Child-Pugh score for chronic liver disease (numeric values not required). (5 marks) Your assessment is consistent with hepatic encephalopathy."},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List 5 potential causes of this exacerbation of her chronic liver disease. For each, state how you will confirm its presence. (5 marks) Cause Confirmatory evidence"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Ascites</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Albumin</div><div class=\"answer-line\">INR</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Encephalopathy</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Bilirubin</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Response / justification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>SBP:</td><td>Ascitic tap for organisms, WCC</td></tr><tr><td>GIT bleed: PR exam for malaena</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Head Trauma ie subdural: CT Head</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Alcohol Intoxication/Withdrawal: Serum ETOH</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Other drug ingestion ie Benzodiazepines: UDS</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Hypoglycaemia: BSL</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Vascular ie portal or hepatic vein thrombosis: USS/ CT Abdo with contrast</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":12,"title":"","stemText":"A 45 year old man has been brought to the ED after falling off a horse.\nAn x-ray of his pelvis is provided in the PROPS BOOKLET.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List 4 significant x-ray findings. (4 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"Classify the injury using the Young-Burgess system. (1 mark)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List 4 potential complications of this injury.\nFor each complication, state:"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"an assessment finding (history, examination or investigation) that would confirm its presence and,"},{"id":"e","label":"Part E","prompt":"the definitive management. (12 marks) Complication Assessment finding Definitive management"}],"propPages":["asset:img53a4a7ed55148a0783"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">1. AP Compression fracture (Open-Book) with:</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Diastasis of symphysis </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Opening of Rt Sacroiliac joints </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Binder too high</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Large amount soft tissue swelling right side</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Management: Reduce Fracture +/- Nerve repair</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Response / justification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Bladder injury</td><td>Contrast extravasation/ Indistinct Bladder dome on RUG</td></tr><tr><td>Management: IDC to decompress, OT for repair</td><td>Urethral Injury</td></tr><tr><td>Blood at meatus/ boggy swelling in perineum</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Inability to pass IDC</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Contrast extravasation on RUG</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Management: Surgical Repair</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Bowel Injury</div><div class=\"answer-line\">PR Bleeding</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part E</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Management: Surgical Repair in OT</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Sacral Plexus injury</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Foot drop/ Incontinence</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Management: Reduce Fracture +/- Nerve repair</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Other answers acceptable</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":13,"title":"","stemText":"A 70 year old man has been brought to the ED by his daughter. He has had increasing confusion for the last week. He lives alone and is unable to give any useful history. His documented medical history includes hypertension, atrial fibrillation and ischaemic heart disease. His documented medications are: indapamide, Ramipril, atorvastatin, metoprolol and warfarin. A CT scan of his head is provided in the PROPS BOOKLET.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"State your diagnosis, with 4 supportive findings demonstrated in the image provided. (5 marks) Diagnosis Supportive findings"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List 5 further investigations you would perform on this man. Provide justification for each. (5 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"State your management with regards to warfarin reversal. Include your end-point. (2 points)"}],"propPages":["asset:img47d087d5d84b16b92b"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Extra-axial collection</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Crossing suture lines</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Sulcal Effacement</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Midline Shift </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Subfalcine Herniation</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><thead><tr><th>Investigation</th><th>Justification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>INR – assess capacity for withdrawal</td><td>Electrolytes – exclude hyponatraemia</td></tr><tr><td>FBE – exclude thrombocytopaenia</td><td>ECG – assess AF control given confusion and possible compliance issues</td></tr><tr><td>Renal Function – exclude renal injury given confusion and possible compliance issues, presence of</td><td>nephrotoxic meds</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Prothrombin Complex Concentrate (Prothrombinex or equivalent) 50u/kg</div><div class=\"answer-line\">End-Point INR &lt;1.5</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":14,"title":"","stemText":"A 25 year old woman has presented with a fever 10 days following a renal transplant.\nHer other medical history includes systemic lupus erythematosus.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List 6 likely causes of her fever. For each, state the investigative finding that would\nsupport its presence. (12 marks) Cause Investigative finding Cause Investigative finding Cause Investigative finding Cause Investigative finding Cause Investigative finding Cause Investigative finding"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><thead><tr><th>Cause</th><th>Distinguishing feature</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CNS infection (ie Aspergillus, cryptococcus) – CSF pleocytosis, organisms, high opening pressure</td><td>Pulmonary infection ie CMV, Pneumocystis  - dyspnea, hypoxia, pulmonary infiltrates on CXR</td></tr><tr><td>Surgical Site Infection –fluctuant collection on USS of transplant kidney or in wound area</td><td>Pyelonephritis of transplant kidney –organisms on urine micro</td></tr><tr><td>Gastrointestinal infection post antibiotics ie C Difficile –  C.Diff toxin on stool</td><td>Acute rejection (ie inadequate suppression)  - acute renal failure</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":15,"title":"","stemText":"A 45 year old woman has presented with intermittent dizziness for the last few hours.\nShortly after arrival, the patient had an ECG recorded during a prolonged dizzy spell. The ECG is provided in the PROPS BOOKLET.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"State your diagnosis. (1 mark)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List 3 supportive features from the ECG. (3 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List 5 potential causes of this problem. (5 marks)\nThe patient has the following vital signs:\n\nGCS\nE4 V4 M6\n\nBP\n75/45 mmHg\n\nRR\n/min O2 sats 99% room air The patient has been moved to a resuscitation room."},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"State 3 components of your initial management. (3 marks)\n1) ________________________________________________________________________________ 2) ________________________________________________________________________________ 3) ________________________________________________________________________________ Your initial management was successful, however the patient continues to have recurrent episodes\n• f the same arrhythmia.\nThe patient remains conscious."},{"id":"e","label":"Part E","prompt":"List 2 options for the subsequent management of this situation. Provide brief details for\neach option. (4 marks) Option Details"}],"propPages":["asset:img64ba5966f64032dd9c"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Polymorphic VT (TdP)</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Very Broad QRS 200ms</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Tachycardia rate (36 x 6 = 216/min)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Shifting axis</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Electrolytes – hypoK, hypoMg</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Drugs ie Sotalol</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Congenital ie long QT syndrome</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Infiltrative ie  Cardiac Amyloid</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Structural ie Dilated Cardiomyopathy</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Ischaemia</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Cardioversion (synchronized) – 200J</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Magnesium 10mmol over 5 mins</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Fix cause eg hypoK</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part E</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Chemical pacing – isoprenaline infusion 5-30mcg/min, aiming for HR &gt;100/min</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Electrical overdrive pacing – synchronized pacing at rate 100/min, increase current to achieve</div><div class=\"answer-line\">electrical and mechanical capture</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":16,"title":"","stemText":"A 65 year old woman has presented with difficulty breathing. She has been unwell for 2 days with increasing shortness of breath and cough, productive of green sputum. She has a history of smoking and chronic obstructive airways disease. An arterial blood gas has been taken and is provided in the PROPS BOOKLET.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"State 2 conclusions from the results provided. Provide evidence for each conclusion.\n(4 marks) 1)________________________________________________________________________________ 2) ________________________________________________________________________________ On examination, the patient is severely short of breath with generalised wheeze and globally decreased breath sounds.\nHer vital signs are:\n\nGCS\nPulse 110 /min\n\nBP\n135/80 mmHg Temperature 38.2 degrees"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List 4 components of your management. For each component, provide details of your\ninitial strategy. (8 marks) Management Details"}],"propPages":["asset:imgf42a28c0d7f9c7df60"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Response / justification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Conclusion 1: There is acute on chronic respiratory failure with elevated CO2 and low pH, however</td><td>shift is less than predicted if all acute, and HCO3 elevated suggesting chronic compensation.</td></tr><tr><td>Conclusion 2: She has a large A-a gradient with PAlv O2 of 266 and gradient of approx. 170</td><td>(elevated) – indicative of shunt.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Response / justification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ventilation – commence BiLevel NIV with initial settings something sensible ie IPAP 10-15, EPAP 5-</td><td>8cmH2O, FiO2 titrated to sats of 88-92%, titrate NIV to work of breathing, improved CO2 clearance</td></tr><tr><td>and minute ventilation.</td><td>Antibiotics – emipiric cover for CAP ie Ceftriaxone IV 1gm + Azithromycin IV 500mg</td></tr><tr><td>Steroids – Hydrocortisone 4mg/kg IV or equivalent PO/alternate agent</td><td>Fluids – not unreasonable, unlikely to be able to tolerate diet on NIV – IV  N/Saline 10ml/kg, repeat</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":17,"title":"","stemText":"A 47 year old man has presented with low back pain of 3 days duration. Your initial assessment is that there is no intercurrent illness. You are considering the role of MRI in this patient.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List 4 indications for MRI from your history. (4 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List 4 indications for MRI from your examination. (4 marks)\nYour assessment is consistent with uncomplicated low back pain of muscular origin. MRI is not indicated. You have elected to admit the patient to your short stay unit."},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"State 4 benefits of short stay admission when compared with discharge home. (4 marks)\n1) ________________________________________________________________________________ 2) ________________________________________________________________________________ 3) ________________________________________________________________________________ 4) ________________________________________________________________________________"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">IVDU</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Immunosuppression</div><div class=\"answer-line\">History of faecal incontinence or urinary retention</div><div class=\"answer-line\">New LL weakness/ paralysis</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">New motor deficit</div><div class=\"answer-line\">New sensory deficit</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Asymptomatic urinary retention on bladder scan</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Lax anal tone</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Fever</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Allows prolonged period of observation (up to 24hrs vs 4hrs)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Allows titration of oral medication</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Allows for allied health input ie Physiotherapy</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Allows for inpatient consultation to facilitate further outpatient review or investigation, or to assist</div><div class=\"answer-line\">in discharge planning</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":18,"title":"","stemText":"A 3 year old child has been brought to the ED with pain in the right ear for the last 2 days. A clinical photo is provided in the PROPS BOOKLET.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"State your diagnosis. Include 3 supportive findings from the clinical photo. (4 marks) Diagnosis Supportive findings"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"State 3 options for analgesia, with doses. (3 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List 3 indications for antibiotic treatment of this condition. (3 marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"List 3 potential complications of this condition. (3 marks)"}],"propPages":["asset:imge75a8c2e4e0712dd01"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Acute suppurative Otitis Media</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Bulging tympanic membrane</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Erythematous tymopanic membrane</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Pus visible behind TM</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Paracetamol 15mg/kg</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Ibuprofen 10mg/kg</div><div class=\"answer-line\">I/N Fentanyl 1.5mcg/kg</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Anything else sensible ie PO oxycodone 0.05 - 1mg/kg</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Prior hearing impairment</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Failure of conservative Rx (ie worsening Symptoms at 48hrs)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Cochlear Implant</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Immunosuppression</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Tympanic Membrane Perforation</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Mastoiditis</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Cholesteatoma</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Intracranial Extension</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Chronic Otitis media with effusion</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Hearing loss</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":19,"title":"","stemText":"A 60 year old Indigenous man has presented with lethargy.\nHe has end-stage renal failure due to type 2 diabetes mellitus. He has missed his last three sessions of haemodialysis.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List 4 indications for emergent haemodialysis in this man. (4 marks)\nYou note that the patient attended ED twice in the last week but discharged against medical advice\n• n both occasions."},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List 4 strategies recommended by ACEM in the Statement on Culturally Competent Care\nand Cultural Safety that could be employed to ensure culturally safe patient-centred care for this man. (4 marks) 1) ________________________________________________________________________________ 2) ________________________________________________________________________________ 3) ________________________________________________________________________________ 4) ________________________________________________________________________________ An ECG was taken and is provided in the PROPS BOOKLET."},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"State the main pathology with 3 supportive features on the ECG. (4 marks) Main pathology Supportive features"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"In the following table, list 4 medications you could use to manage the pathology identified\n• n the ECG prior to haemodialysis. For each, state the mechanism of action and expected\ntime to onset. (12 marks) Medication Mechanism of action Time to onset of action"}],"propPages":["asset:img38fb8fb96023f28049"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Refractory hyperkalaemia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Uraemic Encephalopathy</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Volume Overload</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Refractory Acidosis</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Uraemic pericarditis</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Taking a Cultural History</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Incorporating culturally diverse health beliefs and priorities into ED care and management plans</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Access to support people based on cultural needs</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Ability to speak to a cultural/religious representative of their choosing</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Access to professional interpreter services</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Establishing effective relationships with local primary health carers who service culturally diverse</div><div class=\"answer-line\">populations</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Feedback mechanisms for consumer engagement for those from culturally diverse backrounds</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Department fosters a work ethic of reflection and non-judgemental review of approach to cultural</div><div class=\"answer-line\">safety and competency</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Loss of P waves</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Very broad QRS</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Tall T waves</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Bizarre morphology with atypical LBBB</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Response / justification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Salbutamol – acts on b-receptors to stimulate K+ reuptake – minutes</td><td>Insulin – facilitates increased uptake of K into cells – 15 - 60 minutes</td></tr><tr><td>NaHCO3 – Alkalosis shifts K directly into cells through action on H+-K+ channels - minutes</td><td><strong>Calcium</strong> – Stabilises cardiac membrane – protects against hyperkalaemic effect -seconds - minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":20,"title":"","stemText":"A 22 year old woman has presented with left lower quadrant abdominal pain.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List 4 features on history that would raise suspicion of Pelvic Inflammatory Disease (PID). (4 marks) You suspect she has PID."},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List 4 investigations you may perform. Provide the justification for each. (4 marks)\nInvestigation Justification Your assessment is consistent with uncomplicated PID. The patient is suitable for outpatient management."},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"State 4 components in the management of this patient. (4 marks)\n1) ________________________________________________________________________________ 2) ________________________________________________________________________________ 3) ________________________________________________________________________________ 4) ________________________________________________________________________________"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Vaginal Discharge</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Prior PID</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Multiple sexual partners</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Unprotected sex</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Presence of recently inserted IUD</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Dyspareunia</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><thead><tr><th>Investigation</th><th>Justification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Urine PCR – sensitive for chlamydial and gonococcal infections,</td><td>High-vaginal swabs – allows culture for STI organisms</td></tr><tr><td>b-HCG – pregnancy for both differential diagnosis, and also altering antimicrobial prescribing</td><td>Pelvic USS – exclude tubo-ovarian abscess</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Analgesia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Sexual health/ Infection Control follow up</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Counselling re: safe sexual practice</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Contact Tracing information</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":21,"title":"","stemText":"You are expecting the arrival of a 2 year old boy who has been found unconscious at the bottom of a pool. The child was in cardio-respiratory arrest on arrival of the ambulance. Initial resuscitation measures have achieved return of circulation.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"In the table provided, list 4 complications you will prepare to manage in the ED. For each, state your management. (8 marks) Complication Management The child’s parents have arrived soon after the ambulance."},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List 3 benefits to parental presence during the ongoing resuscitation of this patient.\n(3 marks) 1) ________________________________________________________________________________ 2) ________________________________________________________________________________ 3) ________________________________________________________________________________"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Response / justification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Hypoxic Brain Injury-  sedate, prevent secondary injury ie ETCO2 normal, Sats &gt;92%, Maintain</td><td>cerebral perfusion</td></tr><tr><td>Hypercapnoea – ventilate to normal CO2</td><td>Pool water Pulmonary Aspiration – PEEP and consider antibiotic prophylaxis</td></tr><tr><td>Hypothermia –  active warming to Temp &gt;33 degrees, passive to T &lt;36</td><td>Metabolic Acidosis – volume resuscitation, restore normal haemodynamics</td></tr><tr><td>Cervical Spine Injury – immobilize until radiologic clearance, consider MRI</td><td>Hypoglycaemia – correct with parenteral glucose 2-5ml/kg of 10% dextrose</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Demonstrates resuscitative efforts of medical team</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Can provide important patient information (history, meds, allergies etc)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Can aid in grieving process if resuscitation unsuccessful</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":22,"title":"","stemText":"An 18 month old boy has been referred to the ED with general lethargy.\nThe patient’s GP had performed a Haemoglobin level which was found to be:\n\nHb\n62 g/L (reference range 110 - 150 g/L)","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List 6 potential causes for this result. For each, list one supportive finding you would seek from a full blood count (FBC) or film. (12 marks) Cause Finding from FBC or film"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><thead><tr><th>Cause</th><th>Distinguishing feature</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Iron Deficiency – microcytic, hypochromic; target cells, pencil cells</td><td>Acute Leukaemia – blasts on film</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":23,"title":"","stemText":"The ambulance service has called to notify you of the impending arrival of a 65 year old man with Acute Pulmonary Oedema.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List 4 potential precipitants you will seek on assessment. (4 marks)\nOn arrival, the patient is severely short of breath. Your assessment is consistent with Acute Pulmonary Oedema. The patient has the following vital signs:\n\nGCS\nPulse 110 /min\n\nBP\n210/110 mmHg\n\nRR\n/min O2 sats 88% 15L O2 via non-rebreather mask"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"Complete the tables for the 2 key aspects of your treatment. (8 marks) Treatment Details of initial prescription Mechanism of action End-point Treatment Details of initial prescription Mechanism of action End-point"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Acute Ischaemia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Arrythmia (ie new AF, AV block)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Medication changes/compliance issues</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Electrolyte derangement (ie hypokalaemia)</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">NIV – CPAP 10cm H20 – mechanism – preload reduction through impeding SVC/IVC emptying into Rt</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Heart (secondary effect of improving gas exchange at alveolar-capillary membrane – End-Point SBP</div><div class=\"answer-line\">&lt;140 or similar/ decreased resp distress/ normoxia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">GTN infusion – 10-50mcg/min IV -  mechanism – vasodilation and preload reduction (mild benefit on</div><div class=\"answer-line\">myocardial work/oxygenation through afterload and vasodilatory effects) – end-point – SBP</div><div class=\"answer-line\">&lt;140mmHg</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":24,"title":"","stemText":"A 50 year old woman has presented with a painful, red foot.\nShe has a past medical history of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus, hypertension and smoking. A clinical photo is provided in the PROPS BOOKLET.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List 4 findings from this photo. (4 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"State your clinical impression. (2 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List 6 investigations you might perform on this patient. Provide justification for each.\n(6 marks) 1) ________________________________________________________________________________ 2) ________________________________________________________________________________ 3) ________________________________________________________________________________ 4) ________________________________________________________________________________ 5) ________________________________________________________________________________ 6) ________________________________________________________________________________"}],"propPages":["asset:imgba53ddc67e14270e8d"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Necrotic 2nd toe</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Erythema with ?deroofed blister or desquamation of dorsum of mid/forefoot</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Cellulitis extending into 1st and third toes</div><div class=\"answer-line\">pus</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Diabetic foot infection</div><div class=\"answer-line\">evidence of gangrene of 2nd toe</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><thead><tr><th>Investigation</th><th>Justification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>CT Angio of leg</strong> – define ischaemic status</td><td>XR Foot – exclude gas gangrene</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Wound -swab</strong> – for directed Antimicrobial therapy</td><td>Ankle-brachial index – define macrovascular insufficiency</td></tr><tr><td>Blood Culture – direct antimicrobials</td><td>CXR – pre-op given age and medical comorbidities</td></tr><tr><td>BSL – to optimize BSL control</td><td>Renal function – given diabetes – may impact antimicrobial strategy</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":25,"title":"","stemText":"A 45 year old man has been brought to your tertiary ED. The patient had a high speed motor vehicle accident and was initially managed at a rural hospital prior to helicopter retrieval. A chest x-ray was taken on arrival and has been provided in the PROPS BOOKLET.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List 4 injuries evident on the x-ray. Provide radiographic evidence for each. (8 marks)\nInjury Radiographic evidence The patient is profoundly hypoxic."},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"State 3 measures you would institute to improve the patient’s oxygenation. (3 marks)\n1) ________________________________________________________________________________ 2) ________________________________________________________________________________ 3) ________________________________________________________________________________"}],"propPages":["asset:img201cd4552e08b9075f"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Response / justification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Multiple displaced Rib Fractures – displaced rib fractures seen posteriorly</td><td><strong>Rt TENSION Pneumothorax</strong> – expansion Rt hemithorax, deviation NGT to left, visible lung edge,</td></tr><tr><td>subcut emphysema</td><td>Rt Pulmonary Contusion – Increased opacification throughout Rt lung field</td></tr><tr><td>Lt Pneumothorax – subcutaneous emphysema</td><td>Left Clavicle fracture – comminution of mid-clavicle</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Rt thoracostomy tube</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Lt Thoracostomy tube</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Increase FiO2</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Increase PEEP</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":26,"title":"","stemText":"A 28 year old man has presented after falling on to his right arm while playing basketball.","parts":[{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"State the two major abnormalities on the x-rays. (2 marks) 1) ________________________________________________________________________________ 2) ________________________________________________________________________________"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List 3 potential immediate complications of this injury. For each, state the examination\nfindings that would indicate its presence. (6 marks) Complication Examination findings Your assessment reveals no immediate complications."},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"State 3 components of your management of this patient. (3 marks)\n1) ________________________________________________________________________________ 2) ________________________________________________________________________________ 3) ________________________________________________________________________________"}],"propPages":["asset:img799234e2df9c56f944","asset:imga646e2ad26aac43ef9"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"removedNonQuestionResponseFragments":["rays (AP and lateral) are provided in the PROPS BOOKLET."],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Rt posterior Elbow dislocation</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Displaced fracture of radial head</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Response / justification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Major peripheral nerves – ulnar, radial, median, P.I.N., appropriate motor/sensory exam</td><td>Brachial Artery dissection/ kinking – peripheral pulse deficit, delayed CRT</td></tr><tr><td>Ligamentous Injury – Medial /Collateral Ligamentous Injury - ?tough in exam/ laxity on testing whilst</td><td>under anaesthesia</td></tr><tr><td>Compartment syndrome – compartment pressure high, vascular / sensation deficits</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Reduction under procedural sedation</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Plaster immobilization</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Orthopaedic review to facilitate timely fixation</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":27,"title":"","stemText":"A 45 year old woman has presented with a headache.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List 4 features of your history that would be consistent with the diagnosis of migraine.\n(4 marks) Your assessment is consistent with migraine as the cause of the patient’s symptoms."},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List 3 first line treatment options, each from different pharmacological classes. Include\ndoses. (3 marks) Your first line management has been unsuccessful."},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List 2 options for treating intractable migraine. Include doses. (2 marks)\nAfter a period of observation, the patient has greatly improved. On further questioning, it has become evident that the patient has been suffering from frequent migraines. She has enquired about migraine prophylaxis."},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"List 3 drugs that can be used for migraine prophylaxis. (3 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Unilateral</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Throbbing nature</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Preceeding Aura/ scotoma</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Nausea</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Hx of similar headaches</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Phono/photophobia</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Metoclopramide 10mg IV</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Prochlorperazine 12.5mg IM or PO 5-10mg</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Aspirin 900mg</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Paracetamol 1gm</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Sumatriptan 50-100mg PO or 20mg Intranasal or 6mg subcut</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Steroid controversial (but recommended as 2nd line in pregnancy – prednisolone 50mg</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Sumatriptan 6mg subcut</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Ketorolac IMI 30mg</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Chlorpromazine 12.5mg IV</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Amitryptilline 10mg nocte</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Candesartan 4mg daily</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Pizotifen 0.5mg nocte</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Propranolol 20mg nocte</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Na Valproate 200mg nocte</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Verapamil SR 90mg</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false}]);
